## Start here

Run every backend command through **uv** (`uv run …`): the `terp` CLI and the
project's Python live in the local `.venv`, not on the global PATH — a bare `terp`
or `python` will not resolve.

- **`uv run terp guide`** / **`uv run terp guide <topic>`** — the authoring guide.
  Topics: access, capability, changelog, dataview, dependencies, environment, events,
    files, forms, frontend, idempotency, jobs, layouts, leases, migrations, module,
    outbox, ownership, package-boundaries, passwords, permissions, policy, realtime,
    rules, service, soft-delete, tenancy, testing, theming.
- **`uv run terp --version`** — which platform this app is on, and a named warning
  when the lockstep pins disagree.
- **`uv run terp upgrade --check`** — whether a newer release covers the *whole*
  lockstep set, and the bump recipe. Read `uv run terp guide changelog` first: a
  green gate proves the upgrade did not break the app, not that the release
  changed nothing the app should adopt.
- **`uv run terp inspect capabilities`** — every maintained `terp-cap-*` package:
  what this app already has, what it can adopt, the exact `uv add` line and the
  composition-root wiring. **Read this before building infrastructure by hand** —
  durable delivery, realtime push, background jobs, scheduling, file storage,
  multi-tenancy, signed webhooks and shared multi-replica state are already solved.
- **`uv run terp verify`** — the one-command gate: runs the declared verification
  profile (`--profile quick` for per-edit static checks, `--profile full` for the
  merge bar CI enforces, `--profile release` before shipping). `--list` shows what a
  profile runs; `--format json` emits the machine envelope with every check's
  Terp Standard check report embedded.
- **`uv run terp check`** / **`uv run pytest`** — the gate is the source of truth.
  Green means compliant; red names the fix. `uv run terp check --format json` emits
  structured violations (rule, file, line, message, and the `uv run terp guide` fix
  recipe) for tools and agents; `--format check-report` emits the Terp Standard
  check-report document (catalog-attributed findings + the evaluated-rule inventory).
- **`uv run terp dev`** — run the API (:8000) and frontend (:5173) together, with an
  OpenAPI preflight so the typed client stays current.
- **`uv run terp new module <name>`** — scaffold the five slots.
  **`uv run terp inspect control-plane`** — your roles, permissions, and per-module
  authority map.

## Runtime environment variables

The app declares its own run-time variables in the checked-in
`environment.schema.json`; Studio renders exactly those declarations into the
per-environment `.app.env`. When a feature adds or removes a backend run-time
variable, update the manifest in the same change:

- Preserve existing declarations and declare only variables the app actually reads.
- Names are UPPER_SNAKE (`^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{0,63}$`). Never shadow platform-owned
  `SECRET_KEY`, `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`, `DATABASE_URL`, `ENVIRONMENT`, `WEB_PORT`, or
  `BACKEND_CORS_ORIGINS`; never put frontend build-time `VITE_*` values here.
- Mark tokens, passwords, API keys and client secrets with `"format": "secret"`.
  Never write secret values into the manifest, `.env`, source, tests, prompts or logs.
- **Never also list a declared variable in a compose `environment:` block.** Compose
  resolves `environment:` over `env_file:`, so the `.app.env` value would never reach
  the container — in every environment, including one with no `.env` at all, where
  `${VAR:-}` still wins with an empty string. One owner per variable: either the app
  declares it (`.app.env` is its seam) or compose owns it, never both.
  `uv run terp verify --only env-seams` fails on that overlap.
- Set `"resolvedBy"` on any variable holding an address — it cannot be inferred from
  the name or type, and getting it wrong fails at run time far from the cause:
  - `container` — a service on the compose network dials it. Use the **service name**
    (`http://api:8000`). A loopback address here is the container itself.
  - `host` — your shell dials it, outside the network (`http://127.0.0.1:8000`).
  - `browser` — the user's browser is sent there; a host address is correct
    (this is why `OIDC_REDIRECT_URI` is a `.env` forward and not a declaration).
- Do not create/edit `.app.env` in a Studio-managed environment; Studio owns
  environment-specific values and renders it there. Locally, `cp .app.env.example
  .app.env` — running the inner loop on the deployed seam is how divergence surfaces
  here rather than in production.
- Add concise titles/descriptions; use `required` only when the app cannot operate
  without the variable. Validate the JSON and run the relevant gate after editing.
- **Keep every text field to 500 characters** (`type`, `title`, `description`,
  `format`, `group`, `resolvedBy`), and the manifest to 50 variables. Studio's reader
  fails closed on the **whole file**: one over-long `description` and every declaration
  — the app's secrets included — stops being rendered into `.app.env`. A description
  that fully explains a variable is easily past the cap; write the long version here or
  in the code and keep the manifest's to a sentence or two.
  `uv run terp verify --only env-seams` refuses the manifest before Studio has to.

Full explanation of the two seams and their precedence: `uv run terp guide environment`.

## Definition of done

A change is done only when the whole gate is green — never claim success before
it is. One command runs everything:

```bash
uv run terp verify --profile full   # the merge bar: exactly what CI enforces
```

Or run the four halves individually (useful for narrower reruns while iterating):

```bash
uv run pytest                         # backend gate (architecture, migrations, mounted modules)
npm --prefix frontend run typecheck   # frontend types (tsc)
npm --prefix frontend run lint        # frontend boundary lint + budget
npm --prefix frontend run build       # the production build CI ships
```

A frontend module that fails typecheck breaks the app at runtime (a blank page —
Vite refuses the module graph), so a red typecheck means the app does not work,
even if the backend gate passes. Import only what a package really exports:
check the catalog in `node_modules/@terpjs/react-core/README.md` instead of
guessing component names or props.

Two boundaries are never autonomous agent decisions:

- Never add an opt-out marker or raise an escape-hatch budget. Fix the underlying
  violation; if that is impossible, leave the check red and report the exact human
  approval or platform capability required.
- When `uv run terp guide <rule>` says no sanctioned construct exists, stop before
  implementing a forbidden substitute. Do not hide it behind a helper, marker, budget
  change, or static/local replacement that changes the requested behavior.

## Golden rules

1. Table models inherit `BaseTable`; never redeclare id/created_at/updated_at/version.
2. Services subclass `BaseService`; add *always-on* read filters via
   `business_filters()` — it returns a *sequence of SQLAlchemy conditions* that are
   ANDed into every read (e.g. `return (Model.archived == False,)`), never a dict or
   keyword map; never override `base_query`. A *per-request* filter or sort (a query
   parameter) is declared on the service with `filterable` / `sortable` and passed to
   `list(..., filters=..., sort=...)` — an undeclared field is refused, and both
   compose on top of `base_query()`. Never hand-write a `list()` override to do it
   (`uv run terp guide service`).
3. Every write goes through the service; never call `session.add`/`commit` yourself.
4. Each module declares a `ModuleSpec` with a `Policy` (deny-by-default).
5. Routes set `response_model` to a Read DTO (never the table model); paginate with `Page[T]`.
6. Cap every input string with `Field(max_length=...)`.
7. Import only the `terp.core` public surface + declared capabilities.
8. Added or changed a database model? Generate its migration in the same change:
   `uv run terp migrate make <module_name>` (the app refuses to boot behind on
   migrations, and the test gate fails when migrations drift from the models).

## Frontend golden rules

Screens compose the **`@terpjs/react-core` component surface** — catalog in
`node_modules/@terpjs/react-core/README.md`; data collections via `DataView`
(`node_modules/@terpjs/react-core/src/dataview/README.md`); in-terminal recipes via
`uv run terp guide frontend` / `uv run terp guide dataview` / `uv run terp guide forms`.
The boundary lint enforces:
token-styled primitives only (no raw `<button>`/`<input>`/`<select>`/`<textarea>`/
`<table>`/`<dialog>`/`<form>`), the generated typed client only (no raw `fetch` /
`XMLHttpRequest` / `WebSocket` / `EventSource` / `sendBeacon`),
design-token styling with no `style={}` / `className` / module stylesheets (layout via
`Stack` / the page archetypes), in-app links via the router (no raw `<a href="/...">`),
and no deep `@terpjs/*` imports. Every routed view renders a page archetype (`Page` /
`OverviewPage` / `DetailPage` / `HubPage`) — the router refuses an unframed view.
This app also opts into the **`standard` layout contract** (ADR 0079), declared once in
`frontend/layout-contract.json` — the lint rule finds the file and `main.tsx` imports it,
so there is nothing to keep in sync. The same file is where the app's shell shape
(`shell.density` / `shell.navPlacement` / `shell.contentWidth`), its brand mark
(`shell.brand`), its navigation groups (`shell.navGroups`) and the palette it opens on
(`defaultTheme`) are declared; passing any of
them as a bootstrap option as well is refused when the router is composed. The contract itself: each archetype's body slot accepts only the contract's components (hub bodies:
`HubCard`; overview bodies: `DataView` / `ResourceList` / `Stack` / `Card` / `Divider` /
`Text` + framework states; detail bodies: those plus `Grid`, `DetailList` and `Tabs` —
`Grid` is a detail-body component and not an overview one, because an overview body is a
collection and a grid of summary cards is a hub; direct children only, and an allowed
container's subtree is yours), checked at lint time
*and* at runtime — the plain `Page` stays free for bespoke screens, and every failure
message states the fix (`uv run terp guide layouts`).
Security defaults are refused too: `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` and DOM HTML-injection
sinks (render text, or `Markdown` for rich text), `eval()` / `new Function()`,
`javascript:` URLs, and a static `target="_blank"` without `rel="noopener"`.

No lint modes — every violation is an error. The governed opt-out is a justified
`// terp-allow-<rule>: <reason>` marker, counted exactly against
`frontend/escape-hatch-budget.json` (`npm run lint` runs the ratchet).

## Theming, language & the user menu (already wired)

- **Themes** — the token stylesheet carries the palettes `light`, `dark`, `midnight`,
  `twilight` and `contrast`; the **shell header's** theme toggle offers those plus `system`
  (follow the viewer's platform preference) and switches `<html data-theme>`. Because all
  styling is token-driven, modules never need theme-specific code.
  - **To change how this app looks**, redefine tokens in `frontend/src/theme.css` — it
    loads after the framework's own tokens, so it wins. Per palette, use that palette's
    selector (`[data-theme="dark"] { ... }`). Never inline colours.
  - **To open on a palette other than `light`**, name it as `defaultTheme` in
    `frontend/layout-contract.json`. Do not restyle one palette to imitate another.
  - `uv run terp guide theming` is the full recipe.
- **Languages** — `frontend/src/main.tsx` declares the `locales` catalogs. Add a
  language by adding one entry (`{ label, strings }` overriding the framework strings);
  the user menu then shows a persisted language picker. Keep user-facing text in module
  code as `UiText` props so it follows the switch.
- **User menu** — every app gets the avatar menu (email, role, sign-out) pinned to the
  sidebar bottom automatically; there is nothing to wire. The theme toggle and the
  language switch are separate controls in the shell header, not entries in this menu.

Add a module: `uv run terp new module <name>` → fill the slots → **mount it in
`app/main.py`** (import its `module`, add it to the `modules` list — unmounted
modules serve no routes) → `uv run terp migrate make <name>` →
`uv run terp openapi && npm --prefix frontend run generate` (typed client) →
`uv run terp routes` (route types — see below) → `uv run terp check`.

Route paths and params are type-checked from **generated** types (ADR 0092): the router
is built at runtime from the module manifests, so nothing checks a path or a param name
until `uv run terp routes` extracts them into the committed
`frontend/src/routes.gen.d.ts`. Read and navigate through the checked seams —
`useRouteParams("/records/:recordId")` (exact, per route), `useRouteParam("recordId")`
(one param), and `useTerpNavigate()` for `navigate({ to: "/records/:recordId", params })`
— using the manifest's `:id` spelling. **Regenerate after changing a manifest route**:
`uv run terp verify` refuses a stale table (the `routes-drift` check, before the
typecheck) and prints the command. A route whose `path` is not a plain string literal is
refused by name, because a partial table would make a real path look wrong.
